## Report
Validated. The failpoint is in non-test storage code: `crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:4158-4159` defines `ARGMIN_EXPERIMENTAL_RAFT_EXIT_AFTER_PEER_WAL_BEFORE_RESPONSE`; `append_record_for_log_store` writes and syncs the WAL, syncs the parent, then calls `maybe_exit_after_control_plane_raft_wal_file_sync()` at `crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:4957-4967`; the helper checks only `std::env::var_os(...).is_some()` and calls `std::process::exit(1)` at `crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:5366-5372`. I built the Rust debug target with symbols via Cargo and created a minimal storage example that directly uses the public `ControlPlaneRaftWalFile::append_record` API. Baseline without the env var exited 0 and printed `after append_record`. With `ARGMIN_EXPERIMENTAL_RAFT_EXIT_AFTER_PEER_WAL_BEFORE_RESPONSE=0`, the same binary exited 1 before returning from `append_record` and printed `experimental OpenRaft control-plane exiting after WAL durability before response`. With the env var present but empty, the built binary also exited 1. I attempted Valgrind, but it is not installed in the container (`bash: command not found: valgrind`); ASan via `-Z sanitizer` is unavailable on the stable toolchain. I also captured an LLDB trace: breakpoint at `maybe_exit_after_control_plane_raft_wal_file_sync` was hit, with stack `wal_exit_poc::main` -> `ControlPlaneRaftWalFile::append_record` at line 4899 -> `append_record_for_log_store` at line 4967 -> `maybe_exit_after_control_plane_raft_wal_file_sync` at line 5367. This confirms the process-level exit is reachable through the WAL append path and is controlled solely by environment-variable presence, including value `0`.

